WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.622
In this video, we'll learn
coordinates on a plane,

00:00:03.622 --> 00:00:06.080
a cylinder and a sphere the easy way,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.329
and see how meteorology uses them to
measure the winds round a hurricane's eye,

00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:15.050
and to find distances
and directions on our round Earth.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.750
What are coordinates, and why
do we need more than one kind?

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.575
Let's see what they are, and why
they're built this way.

00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:28.250
Then let's see what they tell
us about storms, maps and winds.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.350
Let's start with a flat map.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.298
To say where something
is, we need a starting point,

00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:37.650
and some directions to count from.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
The starting point is called the origin.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.825
Let's put it at a weather station.

00:00:44.175 --> 00:00:48.750
Now draw one line through it pointing
east, and one pointing north.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.675
These lines are called the axes.

00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:55.516
A weather balloon drifts
three kilometres east and

00:00:55.516 --> 00:00:58.100
four kilometres north of the station.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.875
So we can write its position
as the pair of numbers three and four.

00:01:04.375 --> 00:01:07.266
These two numbers are
called its coordinates,

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:10.350
and together they pin
down one place on the map.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.569
Measuring along straight axes
at right angles, like this,

00:01:14.569 --> 00:01:19.525
is called Cartesian coordinates,
after the French thinker René Descartes.

00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:25.975
Each axis comes with a unit vector: an
arrow of length one that points along it.

00:01:26.325 --> 00:01:29.314
We write x hat
for the arrow pointing east,

00:01:29.314 --> 00:01:32.025
and y hat for the arrow pointing north.

00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:37.475
The balloon's position is three
times x hat, plus four times y hat.

00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:41.950
We met arrows like these
in the vectors movie of this series.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.350
Here's their key property: x hat points
the same way everywhere on the map.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.150
Move the balloon anywhere you
like, and the two arrows don't change.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.625
That's what makes Cartesian
coordinates so easy to work with.

00:01:56.975 --> 00:02:00.575
But many things in the weather
aren't laid out in squares.

00:02:00.925 --> 00:02:06.075
A hurricane spins round its eye, and
a weather radar looks out in rings.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:10.100
For those, there's a better
way to say where something is.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.173
Instead of going east and north, we
can give two other numbers:

00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:18.700
how far away the balloon
is, and in which direction.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.975
The distance from the origin is called r.

00:02:22.325 --> 00:02:27.289
By Pythagoras, r is the square root
of three squared plus four squared,

00:02:27.289 --> 00:02:31.275
which is the square root
of twenty five: five kilometres.

00:02:32.775 --> 00:02:35.925
The direction is an angle, called theta.

00:02:36.275 --> 00:02:39.637
Mathematicians measure
it from the east axis,

00:02:39.637 --> 00:02:42.700
turning anticlockwise as seen from above.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:48.225
Its tangent is four over three, so
theta is about fifty three degrees.

00:02:49.725 --> 00:02:53.391
So the same balloon
is at r equals five kilometres,

00:02:53.391 --> 00:02:56.050
and theta equals fifty three degrees.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.700
These two numbers are
called polar coordinates.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.067
A weather radar measures
exactly this pair:

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:06.925
how far away the raindrops
are, and in which direction.

00:03:07.725 --> 00:03:10.025
Can we go back the other way?

00:03:10.375 --> 00:03:11.725
Yes.

00:03:12.075 --> 00:03:15.550
Think of the right triangle
with the balloon at its tip.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.375
Its long side is r, and its angle
at the station is theta.

00:03:20.725 --> 00:03:24.085
The side going east
is r times cosine theta,

00:03:24.085 --> 00:03:27.675
and the side going north
is r times sine theta.

00:03:28.025 --> 00:03:29.600
Let's check.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:35.417
Five times the cosine of fifty three
degrees is five times zero point six,

00:03:35.417 --> 00:03:36.525
which is three.

00:03:36.875 --> 00:03:39.750
And five times zero point eight is four.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:46.100
So x equals r cosine
theta, and y equals r sine theta.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.875
These two lines are all we need
to switch between the two systems.

00:03:51.675 --> 00:03:54.150
One warning before we go on.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.150
Meteorologists and navigators
measure directions differently.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:04.625
A compass bearing starts from north, and
turns clockwise as seen from above.

00:04:04.975 --> 00:04:08.631
So the balloon's bearing
is ninety minus fifty three,

00:04:08.631 --> 00:04:10.700
which is thirty seven degrees.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.647
Weather reports add one more twist:

00:04:14.647 --> 00:04:18.143
a wind's direction says
where the wind blows from,

00:04:18.143 --> 00:04:20.800
as we saw in the weather basics movie.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:24.050
So a west wind blows towards the east.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.556
Same arrows, different rulers,

00:04:26.556 --> 00:04:30.725
so it's always worth checking
which angle a data set uses.

00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:34.894
Before polar coordinates
can do their real work,

00:04:34.894 --> 00:04:39.175
we need one more building block:
measuring angles in radians.

00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:43.475
Take a circle of radius r, and walk
along its edge.

00:04:43.825 --> 00:04:49.025
The angle in radians is the distance
you walked, divided by the radius.

00:04:49.375 --> 00:04:55.325
So the distance along the edge, called the
arc length, is the radius times the angle.

00:04:55.675 --> 00:05:01.250
A full circle is two pi radians, because
its edge is two pi times the radius.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.250
So one radian is about
fifty seven degrees.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.875
Let's try easy numbers.

00:05:08.225 --> 00:05:11.339
On a ring five kilometres
from the station,

00:05:11.339 --> 00:05:16.409
turning by zero point two radians
walks you five times zero point two,

00:05:16.409 --> 00:05:18.075
which is one kilometre.

00:05:19.575 --> 00:05:24.675
On a ring fifty kilometres out, the same
turn walks you ten kilometres.

00:05:26.175 --> 00:05:29.500
The same angle gives
ten times the distance.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.875
Keep this rule in mind, because it
explains a lot about maps of the Earth.

00:05:35.675 --> 00:05:39.125
Polar coordinates come
with their own unit vectors.

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:44.325
The arrow r hat points straight away
from the origin, the way r grows.

00:05:44.675 --> 00:05:48.726
Theta hat points sideways,
at a right angle to r hat,

00:05:48.726 --> 00:05:52.700
the way theta grows:
anticlockwise, seen from above.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:57.625
At the balloon, r hat
is zero point six, zero point eight.

00:05:57.975 --> 00:06:02.200
And theta hat is minus
zero point eight, zero point six.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.725
Now let's move the balloon
round the circle.

00:06:06.075 --> 00:06:09.925
Its r hat turns with it, and so
does its theta hat.

00:06:10.275 --> 00:06:13.669
That's the big difference
from x hat and y hat:

00:06:13.669 --> 00:06:16.775
polar unit vectors
depend on where you are.

00:06:17.575 --> 00:06:19.650
How fast do they turn?

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.250
Let's turn the balloon's
angle by a small amount, d theta.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.595
The tip of r hat moves
along a circle of radius one,

00:06:28.595 --> 00:06:31.975
so it moves a distance
of one times d theta.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:36.275
And it moves sideways,
in the direction of theta hat.

00:06:36.625 --> 00:06:40.725
So the change in r hat
is theta hat times d theta.

00:06:41.075 --> 00:06:46.650
Dividing by d theta, the rate of change
of r hat with theta is theta hat.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.531
Theta hat turns too, and its tip
moves towards the centre,

00:06:52.531 --> 00:06:55.325
so its rate of change is minus r hat.

00:06:56.825 --> 00:06:59.499
That's all there is to it: turn the angle,

00:06:59.499 --> 00:07:02.300
and each arrow swings
towards the other one.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.875
Now let's describe a wind
in polar coordinates.

00:07:07.225 --> 00:07:12.550
Suppose that at the balloon, a wind
blows due east at ten metres per second.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.600
How much of it blows away from the
station, and how much blows round it?

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:23.132
To find out, we take the dot product
of the wind with each unit vector,

00:07:23.132 --> 00:07:25.175
as in the dot product movie.

00:07:25.525 --> 00:07:28.810
Along r hat, it's ten
times zero point six,

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.400
plus zero times
zero point eight, which is six.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:39.425
Along theta hat, it's ten times minus
zero point eight, which is minus eight.

00:07:40.925 --> 00:07:46.400
So six metres per second blow outward,
and eight metres per second blow round.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:51.975
The minus sign means the wind goes
round clockwise, as seen from above.

00:07:52.325 --> 00:07:56.817
Let's check the length: six squared
plus eight squared is a hundred,

00:07:56.817 --> 00:07:58.600
and its square root is ten.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:03.275
It's the same wind, with new components.

00:08:04.075 --> 00:08:07.500
Now let's take polar
coordinates to a real storm.

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.623
Here is Hurricane Ian
on the twenty eighth of September,

00:08:11.623 --> 00:08:14.385
twenty twenty-two, at six in the evening,

00:08:14.385 --> 00:08:19.775
universal time, an hour before it came
ashore on the southwest coast of Florida.

00:08:20.125 --> 00:08:23.652
The map shows the winds ten
metres above the ground,

00:08:23.652 --> 00:08:28.400
from a weather model analysis with grid
points every three kilometres.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.750
Let's put the origin in the eye, where
the pressure is lowest, and zoom in.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:38.503
The eye sits just off
the coast, over the warm Gulf of Mexico,

00:08:38.503 --> 00:08:42.550
with the flat, low land
of southwest Florida to its east.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.475
Now every wind has two parts.

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:49.675
The radial
wind, along r hat, blows in or out.

00:08:50.025 --> 00:08:54.650
The tangential wind, along
theta hat, blows round the eye.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.345
On a ring twenty five
kilometres from the centre,

00:08:58.345 --> 00:09:02.850
the tangential wind averages about
thirty seven metres per second.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:08.099
That's about a hundred
and thirty kilometres per hour,

00:09:08.099 --> 00:09:13.375
anticlockwise as seen from above, as
in every northern hemisphere hurricane.

00:09:13.725 --> 00:09:18.875
The radial wind averages about
minus twelve and a half metres per second.

00:09:20.375 --> 00:09:24.475
The minus sign means the air
is flowing in, towards the eye.

00:09:24.825 --> 00:09:27.019
The real storm was even stronger:

00:09:27.019 --> 00:09:30.211
the official estimate
of its strongest sustained

00:09:30.211 --> 00:09:32.871
wind at that hour is about a hundred and

00:09:32.871 --> 00:09:37.925
thirty five knots, which is about two
hundred and fifty kilometres per hour.

00:09:38.275 --> 00:09:42.050
The model analysis smooths
the strongest winds.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.275
Now let's zoom back
out to the whole coast.

00:09:46.075 --> 00:09:48.900
Why bother with polar coordinates here?

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.925
Let's go round that ring and plot
the eastward and northward winds.

00:09:54.275 --> 00:09:58.639
Both swing up and down, from
plus to minus, all the way round,

00:09:58.639 --> 00:10:02.300
because the wind keeps
turning as we circle the eye.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:07.550
Now let's plot the tangential
and radial winds round the same ring.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.900
They stay much closer to two steady lines.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.400
Two jumpy curves have become
two fairly steady numbers:

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.625
how fast the storm spins, and
how fast the air flows in.

00:10:19.975 --> 00:10:23.259
The angle at which
the wind crosses each ring,

00:10:23.259 --> 00:10:26.900
called the inflow
angle, is about nineteen degrees.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:32.909
Spin and inflow are exactly what
hurricane scientists care about,

00:10:32.909 --> 00:10:36.100
and polar coordinates
hand them over directly.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.988
The spin also has its own
measure, called vorticity,

00:10:39.988 --> 00:10:43.050
which has its own movie
later in this series.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.700
Here's a surprise.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:50.946
Picture an air parcel, a small volume
of air that moves with the flow,

00:10:50.946 --> 00:10:55.352
going round the eye at a steady
thirty seven metres per second,

00:10:55.352 --> 00:10:57.800
on that twenty five kilometre ring.

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:01.150
Its tangential wind never changes.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.400
Is it accelerating?

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:09.650
Yes, because its direction keeps changing,
and polar coordinates show exactly why.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.513
Its velocity is thirty
seven times theta hat,

00:11:13.513 --> 00:11:17.025
and theta hat turns
as the parcel goes round.

00:11:17.375 --> 00:11:20.514
By the chain rule, from
the derivatives movie,

00:11:20.514 --> 00:11:25.496
the rate of change of theta hat in time
is its rate of change with theta,

00:11:25.496 --> 00:11:28.977
which is minus r hat, times
how fast theta changes,

00:11:28.977 --> 00:11:31.775
which is the speed divided by the radius.

00:11:32.125 --> 00:11:36.791
So the acceleration is minus the speed
squared over the radius,

00:11:36.791 --> 00:11:39.975
times r hat: it points in,
towards the eye.

00:11:41.475 --> 00:11:46.868
With our numbers, it's thirty seven
squared, over twenty five thousand metres,

00:11:46.868 --> 00:11:51.500
which is about zero point zero five five
metres per second squared.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.404
That's almost two hundred
times smaller than gravity,

00:11:56.404 --> 00:11:58.395
but something has to supply it,

00:11:58.395 --> 00:12:03.725
and here it's mostly the pressure force,
because the pressure is lowest in the eye.

00:12:04.075 --> 00:12:08.650
We'll come back to this balance
in the movie on the gradient wind.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.032
The lesson for now:
when unit vectors turn,

00:12:12.032 --> 00:12:15.275
steady components can
still mean acceleration.

00:12:16.075 --> 00:12:18.475
A hurricane isn't flat, though.

00:12:18.825 --> 00:12:24.410
The air spirals in near the sea, rises in
a ring of towering clouds round the eye,

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.612
called the eyewall,
and flows out near the top,

00:12:27.612 --> 00:12:30.200
about twelve to fifteen kilometres up.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:35.400
To describe that, we add a third
coordinate: the height, z, straight up.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:38.105
The distance r, the angle theta and

00:12:38.105 --> 00:12:41.940
the height z together are
called cylindrical coordinates,

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:46.650
because all the places with the
same r form a cylinder round the axis.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.775
The wind now has three parts:
radial, tangential and vertical.

00:12:52.125 --> 00:12:57.700
The vertical unit vector, z hat, is
the same everywhere, just like x hat.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.825
Only r hat and theta hat turn.

00:13:01.175 --> 00:13:06.150
So cylindrical coordinates are just
polar coordinates with a height added.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:10.577
In a hurricane, the radial
wind is negative near the sea,

00:13:10.577 --> 00:13:14.225
flowing in, and positive
near the top, flowing out.

00:13:15.025 --> 00:13:17.925
Let's check that with Ian's real winds.

00:13:18.275 --> 00:13:21.783
Here are five layers
of air round Ian's eye,

00:13:21.783 --> 00:13:25.850
at that same hour, from
the reanalysis called ERA5.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.941
They run from about three hundred metres

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:32.025
above the sea to about
fifteen kilometres up.

00:13:32.375 --> 00:13:37.241
The colour on each layer is the radial
wind: rose, with a minus sign,

00:13:37.241 --> 00:13:41.050
where the air flows
in, and orange where it flows out.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.475
On the lowest layer, the air
flows in almost everywhere.

00:13:45.825 --> 00:13:49.038
On a ring two hundred
kilometres from the eye,

00:13:49.038 --> 00:13:52.250
it averages about minus six
metres per second.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:58.575
At about one and a half kilometres,
the inflow has almost died away.

00:13:58.925 --> 00:14:01.698
At about six kilometres, in the middle,

00:14:01.698 --> 00:14:06.675
the air hardly flows in or out at all;
near the eye, it rises instead.

00:14:07.025 --> 00:14:12.600
Near the top, at about twelve and a half
and fifteen kilometres, the sign flips.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:17.750
On the same ring, the air flows out
at about four metres per second.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:24.850
Let's take a short, closer look, from just
above the sea, up the side of the stack.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:28.850
In at the bottom, up through
the middle, and out at the top.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:32.416
This reanalysis smooths
the strongest winds,

00:14:32.416 --> 00:14:35.925
so we use it for the directions,
not the speeds.

00:14:36.275 --> 00:14:42.075
And every layer is described by the same
three coordinates: r, theta and z.

00:14:42.875 --> 00:14:45.775
Now the biggest shape of all: the Earth.

00:14:46.125 --> 00:14:49.050
Let's put the origin
at the Earth's centre.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:54.197
A point on a sphere is pinned
down by its distance from the centre,

00:14:54.197 --> 00:14:55.700
r, and by two angles.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:59.380
On the Earth's surface,
r is the Earth's radius:

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:02.363
about six thousand four
hundred kilometres,

00:15:02.363 --> 00:15:06.525
or more exactly, six thousand
three hundred and seventy one.

00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:09.400
These are spherical coordinates.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:14.488
Meteorologists use the two angles
you already know from maps:

00:15:14.488 --> 00:15:16.275
latitude and longitude.

00:15:17.075 --> 00:15:20.625
Latitude says how far
north or south a place is.

00:15:20.975 --> 00:15:24.175
Draw a line from the Earth's
centre to the place.

00:15:24.525 --> 00:15:29.600
Latitude is the angle between that
line and the plane of the equator.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:31.700
We write it phi.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:34.600
At the equator, phi is zero.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:38.300
At the North Pole, it's ninety
degrees north.

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:44.125
At the South Pole, it's ninety degrees
south, which we write as minus ninety.

00:15:44.475 --> 00:15:47.396
Brest, a port at the western
tip of France,

00:15:47.396 --> 00:15:50.725
is at about forty
eight point four degrees north.

00:15:51.075 --> 00:15:55.562
All the places with the same latitude
form a circle round the Earth,

00:15:55.562 --> 00:15:56.750
called a parallel.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.971
One caution: many maths and

00:15:58.971 --> 00:16:03.475
physics books measure the angle
down from the North Pole instead.

00:16:03.825 --> 00:16:09.375
That angle is called the colatitude, and
it's ninety degrees minus the latitude.

00:16:09.725 --> 00:16:14.200
So sine and cosine swap places
between the two conventions.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.000
Longitude says how far east or west.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:23.150
Picture the half circles that run
from pole to pole, called meridians.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.575
The one through Greenwich,
in London, is the prime meridian.

00:16:27.925 --> 00:16:30.966
Longitude is the angle
round the Earth's axis,

00:16:30.966 --> 00:16:34.800
from the prime meridian
to the meridian through the place.

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.975
We write it lambda.

00:16:37.325 --> 00:16:40.725
East is positive, and west is negative.

00:16:41.075 --> 00:16:44.363
Brest is at about four
and a half degrees west,

00:16:44.363 --> 00:16:47.650
so its lambda is minus
four point five degrees.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:53.047
Some data sets count longitude from zero
to three hundred and sixty degrees,

00:16:53.047 --> 00:16:55.040
all the way round to the east,

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.825
so Brest becomes three hundred
and fifty five point five.

00:16:59.175 --> 00:17:00.892
The lines of latitude and

00:17:00.892 --> 00:17:05.425
longitude together make a grid
on the globe, called the graticule.

00:17:06.225 --> 00:17:08.975
Where exactly is Brest in space?

00:17:09.325 --> 00:17:14.275
Let's find its position vector, the arrow
from the Earth's centre to Brest.

00:17:14.625 --> 00:17:18.925
We'll do it in two steps, using a right
triangle each time.

00:17:19.275 --> 00:17:22.225
First, look at the Earth from the side.

00:17:22.575 --> 00:17:27.000
The line to Brest makes the angle
phi with the equator plane.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:32.925
So Brest's height above the equator plane
is the Earth's radius times sine phi.

00:17:33.275 --> 00:17:38.550
And its distance from the Earth's
axis is the radius times cosine phi.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:42.200
Second, look down
from above the North Pole.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:48.050
In the equator plane, that distance
from the axis points at the angle lambda.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.656
That's polar coordinates
in a flat plane again,

00:17:51.656 --> 00:17:55.950
so it splits into a cosine lambda
part and a sine lambda part.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:18:02.128
Putting it together: x is the radius
times cosine phi times cosine lambda,

00:18:02.128 --> 00:18:06.144
y is the radius times cosine
phi times sine lambda,

00:18:06.144 --> 00:18:08.900
and z is the radius times sine phi.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:14.355
For Brest, the height above the equator
plane is six thousand three hundred and

00:18:14.355 --> 00:18:18.039
seventy one kilometres times
zero point seven four eight,

00:18:18.039 --> 00:18:22.175
which is about four thousand seven
hundred and sixty kilometres.

00:18:23.675 --> 00:18:28.195
And its distance from the axis
is six thousand three hundred and

00:18:28.195 --> 00:18:31.161
seventy one times zero point six six four:

00:18:31.161 --> 00:18:34.975
about four thousand two
hundred and thirty kilometres.

00:18:36.475 --> 00:18:40.630
That distance from the axis, the
radius times cosine phi,

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:45.150
deserves a second look, because
it's a cylindrical coordinate.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:48.412
The Earth spins once a day round its axis,

00:18:48.412 --> 00:18:52.225
so every place travels
round a circle with that radius.

00:18:52.575 --> 00:18:55.241
The spin rate, called capital Omega,

00:18:55.241 --> 00:19:00.500
is seven point two nine times ten
to the minus five radians per second.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:05.525
The ground's speed is the spin
rate times the radius of its circle.

00:19:05.875 --> 00:19:09.109
At the equator,
the circle has the full radius,

00:19:09.109 --> 00:19:14.063
so the ground moves at seven point two
nine times ten to the minus five,

00:19:14.063 --> 00:19:17.297
times six point three seven one
million metres:

00:19:17.297 --> 00:19:20.875
about four hundred and sixty
five metres per second.

00:19:22.375 --> 00:19:25.197
At Brest, the circle is two thirds as big,

00:19:25.197 --> 00:19:29.900
so the ground moves at about three
hundred and nine metres per second.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:34.965
At Longyearbyen,
on the Arctic islands of Svalbard,

00:19:34.965 --> 00:19:38.950
seventy eight degrees
north, it's only about ninety five.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.800
And at the poles, it's zero.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:46.751
This shrinking circle is part
of the reason the Earth's

00:19:46.751 --> 00:19:50.287
spin affects winds differently
at different latitudes,

00:19:50.287 --> 00:19:54.150
as we saw in the cross product
movie on our spinning Earth.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:58.025
Now let's measure distances on the sphere.

00:19:58.375 --> 00:20:00.293
Going north along a meridian,

00:20:00.293 --> 00:20:04.657
you walk on a circle whose radius
is the full radius of the Earth,

00:20:04.657 --> 00:20:08.625
because every meridian is half
of a circle round the centre.

00:20:08.975 --> 00:20:10.871
So by the arc length rule,

00:20:10.871 --> 00:20:16.850
the distance north is the Earth's radius
times the change in latitude, in radians.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.800
Let's find the length of one degree.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:23.536
One degree is pi over one
hundred and eighty,

00:20:23.536 --> 00:20:27.525
which is zero point zero
one seven four five radians.

00:20:27.875 --> 00:20:31.888
Times six thousand three hundred
and seventy one kilometres,

00:20:31.888 --> 00:20:35.700
that's about one hundred
and eleven point two kilometres.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.673
And it's the same at every latitude:

00:20:39.673 --> 00:20:44.000
a degree north is always
about a hundred and eleven kilometres.

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:47.510
One minute of latitude,
a sixtieth of a degree,

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:50.267
is about one point eight five kilometres,

00:20:50.267 --> 00:20:54.100
which is the nautical mile
from the weather basics movie.

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:57.050
Going east is different.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.917
Walking east, you follow a parallel,

00:20:59.917 --> 00:21:04.950
and a parallel is a circle round
the Earth's axis, not round its centre.

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:10.800
Its radius is the one we just found:
the Earth's radius times cosine phi.

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:17.575
So the distance east is the radius, times
cosine phi, times the change in longitude.

00:21:17.925 --> 00:21:20.196
That cosine phi is there because

00:21:20.196 --> 00:21:24.525
the circle you walk on shrinks
as you get closer to the pole.

00:21:24.875 --> 00:21:28.775
Let's compare one degree
of longitude at four places.

00:21:29.125 --> 00:21:33.050
At the equator, cosine phi
is one, so it's a hundred and

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:36.275
eleven kilometres,
the same as a degree north.

00:21:37.775 --> 00:21:41.173
At Brest, cosine phi
is zero point six six,

00:21:41.173 --> 00:21:44.175
so it's about seventy four kilometres.

00:21:45.675 --> 00:21:49.092
At Longyearbyen, cosine
phi is zero point two,

00:21:49.092 --> 00:21:53.325
so one degree east is only
about twenty three kilometres.

00:21:54.825 --> 00:21:58.725
And at the pole, where all
the meridians meet, it's zero.

00:21:59.075 --> 00:22:04.036
Like the segments of an orange, the
meridians are widest apart at the equator,

00:22:04.036 --> 00:22:06.325
and they come together at the poles.

00:22:07.125 --> 00:22:10.800
This matters every time
we use weather data.

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:14.685
ERA5, the reanalysis
we've used in this series,

00:22:14.685 --> 00:22:20.325
gives values on a grid every quarter
of a degree in latitude and longitude.

00:22:20.675 --> 00:22:25.880
Near Brest, one grid box is about twenty
eight kilometres from south to north,

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:30.150
but only about eighteen and a half
kilometres from west to east.

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:35.545
At Svalbard, it's still
twenty eight kilometres tall,

00:22:35.545 --> 00:22:37.750
but under six kilometres wide.

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:42.925
So the boxes get thinner
and thinner towards the poles.

00:22:43.275 --> 00:22:44.500
Forget the cosine,

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:49.672
and every east-west distance near
the poles comes out several times too big,

00:22:49.672 --> 00:22:53.075
and every east-west slope
several times too small.

00:22:53.425 --> 00:22:59.269
A weather model on such a grid also has to
take very short time steps near the poles,

00:22:59.269 --> 00:23:03.600
which is one reason many modern
models use other kinds of grid.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.975
Distances give us areas.

00:23:07.325 --> 00:23:12.467
A small patch of the Earth's surface
is the radius times d phi tall,

00:23:12.467 --> 00:23:16.400
and the radius times cosine
phi times d lambda wide.

00:23:16.750 --> 00:23:23.175
So its area is the radius squared, times
cosine phi, times d phi, times d lambda.

00:23:23.525 --> 00:23:25.950
Here's a lovely consequence.

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:30.925
Let's add up all the patches between
the equator and some latitude.

00:23:31.275 --> 00:23:36.450
Adding up is integrating, and the integral
of cosine phi is sine phi.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:42.000
So the area between the equator
and latitude phi grows like sine phi.

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:46.425
The sine of thirty degrees is one half.

00:23:46.775 --> 00:23:50.728
So half of the northern hemisphere
lies between the equator and

00:23:50.728 --> 00:23:52.045
thirty degrees north,

00:23:52.045 --> 00:23:56.500
and half of the whole Earth lies
between thirty south and thirty north.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:02.725
The tropics and subtropics are
huge, and the polar caps are small.

00:24:03.075 --> 00:24:08.875
That's why a global average temperature
has to weight each latitude by cosine phi.

00:24:09.225 --> 00:24:14.875
Otherwise, the small polar boxes would
count as much as the big tropical ones.

00:24:15.675 --> 00:24:19.075
Now let's stand at a weather
station, at Brest.

00:24:19.425 --> 00:24:25.175
Brest sits at the western tip of Brittany,
with the Atlantic Ocean on three sides.

00:24:25.525 --> 00:24:29.850
A forecaster there doesn't use
axes from the Earth's centre.

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:35.189
She uses three local arrows: i hat
pointing east, j hat pointing north,

00:24:35.189 --> 00:24:39.475
and k hat pointing straight
up, away from the Earth's centre.

00:24:39.825 --> 00:24:43.150
This is the local east, north, up frame.

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:50.150
In it, the wind is u times i hat, plus
v times j hat, plus w times k hat.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:56.800
Here, u is the eastward wind, v the
northward wind, and w the upward wind.

00:24:57.150 --> 00:24:59.552
And i hat cross j hat is k hat,

00:24:59.552 --> 00:25:04.200
so the frame is right-handed,
as in the cross product movie.

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:07.075
Each station has its own frame.

00:25:07.425 --> 00:25:10.175
Let's zoom back out to the whole globe.

00:25:10.525 --> 00:25:15.310
At Trappes, near Paris, four hundred
and eighty kilometres east of Brest,

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:18.850
up points in a direction
about four degrees different.

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:22.442
At Singapore, near the equator,

00:25:22.442 --> 00:25:26.625
up differs from Brest's
up by about a hundred and one degrees.

00:25:28.125 --> 00:25:33.125
So the local arrows change as you
move, just like r hat and theta hat.

00:25:33.475 --> 00:25:35.150
Let's see how.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:39.642
Suppose a plane flies due east
along the parallel through Brest,

00:25:39.642 --> 00:25:42.425
at two hundred and fifty
metres per second.

00:25:42.775 --> 00:25:46.550
In one hour, it covers
nine hundred kilometres.

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:51.520
That's nine hundred divided by seventy
four kilometres per degree:

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:53.900
about twelve degrees of longitude.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:26:00.959
So its east arrow has swung round the
Earth's axis by those same twelve degrees,

00:26:00.959 --> 00:26:04.225
exactly like theta hat
swinging round a circle.

00:26:04.575 --> 00:26:08.145
Going north tips the arrows
too: after a hundred and

00:26:08.145 --> 00:26:13.500
eleven kilometres north, the up arrow
has tilted one degree towards the north.

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:17.499
Written out, the rates
of change of i hat, j hat and

00:26:17.499 --> 00:26:22.272
k hat for a moving air parcel each
have a speed divided by a radius,

00:26:22.272 --> 00:26:25.500
the same pattern we found
round the hurricane.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:29.400
Speed over radius
is how fast the arrows turn.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:32.050
Why does that matter?

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:36.892
Because Newton's law is about
the change of the whole wind vector,

00:26:36.892 --> 00:26:38.525
not just of its numbers.

00:26:38.875 --> 00:26:43.797
When the arrows turn, the change
of the wind has extra pieces,

00:26:43.797 --> 00:26:46.575
even when u, v and w stay the same.

00:26:46.925 --> 00:26:50.892
For the eastward part, it's
the rate of change of u,

00:26:50.892 --> 00:26:55.775
minus u v tangent phi over the
radius, plus u w over the radius.

00:26:56.125 --> 00:26:59.350
Those extra terms are called metric terms.

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:03.475
They come only from the turning
arrows of the coordinates.

00:27:03.825 --> 00:27:05.525
How big are they?

00:27:05.875 --> 00:27:09.739
For a wind of ten metres
per second east and ten north,

00:27:09.739 --> 00:27:15.218
at forty five degrees, u v tangent phi
over the radius is a hundred times one,

00:27:15.218 --> 00:27:17.677
over six point three seven million:

00:27:17.677 --> 00:27:22.875
about one point six times ten to the
minus five metres per second squared.

00:27:24.375 --> 00:27:29.725
That's about sixty five times smaller
than the Coriolis effect on the same wind.

00:27:30.075 --> 00:27:32.665
So in one weather system they're small,

00:27:32.665 --> 00:27:36.450
but across whole oceans
and the whole globe, they add up.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:41.100
We'll meet them again in the movie
on the equations of motion.

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:43.527
Polar, cylindrical and

00:27:43.527 --> 00:27:48.410
spherical coordinates are all
examples of curvilinear coordinates:

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:50.925
coordinates whose lines can curve.

00:27:51.275 --> 00:27:53.400
They share three features.

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:58.675
First, the coordinate lines are
curves, like circles and meridians.

00:27:59.025 --> 00:28:02.875
Second, the unit vectors
change from place to place.

00:28:03.225 --> 00:28:07.513
Third, a change in a coordinate
isn't a distance by itself,

00:28:07.513 --> 00:28:09.475
so it needs a scale factor.

00:28:09.825 --> 00:28:14.636
In polar coordinates, a change
of theta walks you r times d theta,

00:28:14.636 --> 00:28:16.750
so theta's scale factor is r.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:21.600
On the Earth, latitude's scale
factor is the Earth's radius,

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:25.125
and longitude's
is the radius times cosine phi.

00:28:25.475 --> 00:28:29.100
These scale factors show
up whenever we take slopes.

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:33.278
For example, the eastward
slope of the temperature on the

00:28:33.278 --> 00:28:36.491
sphere is one over the
radius times cosine phi,

00:28:36.491 --> 00:28:39.225
times its rate of change with longitude.

00:28:39.575 --> 00:28:44.475
That's part of the gradient, which gets
its own movie, next in this series.

00:28:44.825 --> 00:28:48.870
Weather models even use a curvilinear
vertical coordinate:

00:28:48.870 --> 00:28:52.775
pressure instead of height, which
has its own movie too.

00:28:53.575 --> 00:28:56.650
So why are there so
many coordinate systems?

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.175
There are three good reasons.

00:28:59.525 --> 00:29:01.350
The first is shape.

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:06.868
When the coordinates match the shape
of the problem, the numbers become simple:

00:29:06.868 --> 00:29:10.073
a ring round the eye
is just r equals a constant,

00:29:10.073 --> 00:29:13.475
and the Earth's surface
is just r equals its radius.

00:29:13.825 --> 00:29:16.075
The second is measurement.

00:29:16.425 --> 00:29:22.109
Use the coordinates your instruments use:
a radar sees distance and direction,

00:29:22.109 --> 00:29:24.659
a station feels east, north and up,

00:29:24.659 --> 00:29:28.375
and the data come on a latitude
and longitude grid.

00:29:28.725 --> 00:29:30.650
The third is meaning.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:36.242
The right coordinates split a vector
into the parts that physics cares about:

00:29:36.242 --> 00:29:41.825
spin and inflow round a storm, or along
and across a coastline, as we'll see soon.

00:29:42.175 --> 00:29:48.350
Now let's put these ideas to work on maps,
on a storm surge, and on a moving storm.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:52.650
First, the shortest
way between two places.

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.850
On a flat sheet, it's a straight line.

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:58.956
On a sphere, it's part of a great circle:

00:29:58.956 --> 00:30:04.200
a circle whose centre is the Earth's
centre, like the equator or any meridian.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:07.544
Picture a flat sheet
slicing through London,

00:30:07.544 --> 00:30:09.925
Los Angeles and the Earth's centre.

00:30:10.275 --> 00:30:13.925
Where it cuts the surface
is the great circle route.

00:30:14.275 --> 00:30:16.000
How long is it?

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:21.100
Take the dot product of the two
places' position arrows of length one.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:23.305
As in the dot product movie,

00:30:23.305 --> 00:30:28.075
that's the cosine of the angle between
them, here at the Earth's centre.

00:30:28.425 --> 00:30:31.563
The angle comes out at about
seventy nine degrees,

00:30:31.563 --> 00:30:36.333
and the arc length rule turns it into
about eight thousand seven hundred and

00:30:36.333 --> 00:30:37.400
sixty kilometres.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:44.075
It doesn't head west-south-west,
the way Los Angeles looks on a flat map.

00:30:44.425 --> 00:30:49.169
It leaves London heading north-west,
at a bearing of about three hundred and

00:30:49.169 --> 00:30:52.915
twelve degrees, and climbs
to about sixty two degrees north,

00:30:52.915 --> 00:30:54.975
near the west coast of Greenland.

00:30:56.475 --> 00:31:01.251
That's why flights from Europe
to California pass over Greenland and

00:31:01.251 --> 00:31:02.375
northern Canada.

00:31:03.175 --> 00:31:07.265
Sailors used a different route
for centuries: the rhumb line,

00:31:07.265 --> 00:31:10.550
which keeps the same
compass bearing all the way.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:13.800
It's easy to steer, but it's longer.

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:19.511
From London to Los Angeles, the rhumb line
holds a bearing of about two hundred and

00:31:19.511 --> 00:31:23.839
fifty eight degrees, and it's about
nine thousand seven hundred and

00:31:23.839 --> 00:31:25.325
thirty kilometres long.

00:31:26.825 --> 00:31:30.678
That's about eleven percent
longer than the great circle:

00:31:30.678 --> 00:31:35.275
nearly a thousand kilometres
more, or about an hour of extra flying.

00:31:36.775 --> 00:31:41.500
On the globe, the great circle looks
straight, and the rhumb line curves.

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:45.800
On the most famous flat
map, it's the other way round.

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:47.850
Let's see why.

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.587
Flattening the globe
always stretches something:

00:31:51.587 --> 00:31:55.625
you can't flatten an orange peel
without tearing or stretching it.

00:31:55.975 --> 00:32:02.175
The simplest map just uses longitude
as x and latitude as y, on squares.

00:32:02.525 --> 00:32:05.200
That's how most weather data arrive.

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:10.300
But on this map, every parallel
is drawn as long as the equator.

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:16.475
So at latitude phi, east-west distances
are stretched by one over cosine phi.

00:32:16.825 --> 00:32:22.175
At Brest, that's one over zero point six
six, about one and a half times.

00:32:23.675 --> 00:32:26.775
At Svalbard, it's almost five times.

00:32:28.275 --> 00:32:31.208
North-south distances
aren't stretched at all,

00:32:31.208 --> 00:32:33.950
so shapes near the poles
are squashed flat.

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:39.225
In fifteen sixty nine,
Gerardus Mercator fixed the shapes.

00:32:39.575 --> 00:32:43.834
His idea: if east-west
is stretched by one over cosine phi,

00:32:43.834 --> 00:32:46.650
stretch north-south by the same amount.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:49.349
Then a small circle stays a circle,

00:32:49.349 --> 00:32:53.375
and a small angle on the map
is the true angle on the Earth.

00:32:53.725 --> 00:32:56.450
Maps like this are called conformal.

00:32:56.800 --> 00:33:01.473
That's exactly what a navigator wants:
a line of constant bearing,

00:33:01.473 --> 00:33:05.225
the rhumb line, is a straight
line on a Mercator map.

00:33:05.575 --> 00:33:10.058
To build it, each small step
north, the radius times d phi,

00:33:10.058 --> 00:33:13.250
is drawn one over cosine phi times longer.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:18.474
Adding up all those steps gives
the height on the map: the Earth's radius,

00:33:18.474 --> 00:33:23.875
times the natural log of the tangent of
forty five degrees plus half the latitude.

00:33:24.225 --> 00:33:27.324
You can check it
with the derivatives movie:

00:33:27.324 --> 00:33:31.550
its slope with respect to phi
is the radius over cosine phi.

00:33:33.050 --> 00:33:36.512
Near the poles, that height
runs off to infinity,

00:33:36.512 --> 00:33:39.550
so a Mercator map can
never show the poles.

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:43.475
But conformal has a price: areas.

00:33:43.825 --> 00:33:47.543
Stretched by one over cosine
phi in both directions,

00:33:47.543 --> 00:33:50.975
an area is stretched by
one over cosine squared.

00:33:51.325 --> 00:33:54.375
At sixty degrees, that's four times.

00:33:55.875 --> 00:33:59.175
At Svalbard, it's about twenty four times.

00:34:00.675 --> 00:34:05.475
So on a Mercator map, Greenland
looks about as big as Africa.

00:34:05.825 --> 00:34:09.775
In truth, Africa is about
fourteen times bigger.

00:34:11.275 --> 00:34:14.381
The Mercator map is great
for directions and

00:34:14.381 --> 00:34:17.275
shapes, and terrible for comparing sizes.

00:34:18.075 --> 00:34:21.225
For climate maps, sizes matter more.

00:34:21.575 --> 00:34:25.616
If we want to know how much
of the Earth is warmer than usual,

00:34:25.616 --> 00:34:29.200
a map that inflates the polar
regions would mislead us.

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:34.975
So climate scientists often use
equal-area maps, like the Equal Earth map.

00:34:35.325 --> 00:34:38.679
On it, every patch keeps
its true relative area,

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:43.500
so Greenland is shown at its real
size, about a fourteenth of Africa.

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:48.075
The price now is shape: towards
the poles, shapes get skewed.

00:34:48.425 --> 00:34:52.375
At sixty degrees north, away
from the middle of this map,

00:34:52.375 --> 00:34:55.425
angles can be off by thirty
degrees or more.

00:34:56.925 --> 00:35:01.375
No flat map can keep both areas
and shapes right everywhere.

00:35:01.725 --> 00:35:04.875
So the rule is: pick the map for the job.

00:35:05.225 --> 00:35:08.346
Use conformal maps
for winds and directions,

00:35:08.346 --> 00:35:11.325
and equal-area maps for comparing amounts.

00:35:11.675 --> 00:35:16.125
We'll read many maps like these
in the movie on reading weather maps.

00:35:16.925 --> 00:35:22.275
There's one more map that every forecaster
knows: the view from above a pole.

00:35:22.625 --> 00:35:24.982
On it, the pole sits in the middle,

00:35:24.982 --> 00:35:28.350
and the circles of latitude
become rings round it.

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:30.768
So it's polar coordinates again:

00:35:30.768 --> 00:35:35.290
the distance from the middle stands
for how far you are from the pole,

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:37.875
and the angle round it is the longitude.

00:35:38.225 --> 00:35:42.206
Here is the height of the ten
hectopascal pressure surface,

00:35:42.206 --> 00:35:45.377
about thirty kilometres
up in the stratosphere,

00:35:45.377 --> 00:35:48.750
on the twenty first of January,
twenty twenty six.

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:53.325
The lines join places where that
surface has the same height.

00:35:53.675 --> 00:35:56.485
They circle the Arctic:
about twenty eight and

00:35:56.485 --> 00:35:58.868
a half kilometres where they're lowest,

00:35:58.868 --> 00:36:02.350
and almost thirty one kilometres
at thirty degrees north.

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:09.475
The winds blow along these lines, round
and round the Arctic, from west to east.

00:36:09.825 --> 00:36:15.450
This great winter whirl of cold air
is called the stratospheric polar vortex.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:21.375
In this view, the eastward wind
is the tangential wind round the pole.

00:36:21.725 --> 00:36:25.917
And the northward wind is the radial
wind with a minus sign,

00:36:25.917 --> 00:36:29.900
because blowing north means
blowing in, towards the pole.

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:33.132
Westerly winds, the winds from the west,

00:36:33.132 --> 00:36:36.950
turn anticlockwise as seen
from above the North Pole.

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:40.788
So the polar vortex is like
the hurricane's spin,

00:36:40.788 --> 00:36:43.350
only far wider and slower to change.

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:47.563
Unlike a hurricane,
though, it isn't fed by a warm sea:

00:36:47.563 --> 00:36:52.550
it forms because the polar stratosphere
cools in the long winter night.

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:55.852
And look where its centre is:
not at the pole,

00:36:55.852 --> 00:37:01.050
but at almost seventy three degrees north,
over the Barents Sea, north of Russia.

00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:06.680
If we put the origin
at the vortex's own centre instead,

00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:09.925
its winds become far
more purely tangential.

00:37:10.275 --> 00:37:14.275
Choosing the origin is part
of choosing good coordinates.

00:37:14.625 --> 00:37:18.779
When the vortex weakens
or splits, cold air can spill south,

00:37:18.779 --> 00:37:21.825
which is a story
for the stratosphere movie.

00:37:22.625 --> 00:37:26.389
Now let's change coordinates
without moving the origin:

00:37:26.389 --> 00:37:28.100
we'll just turn the axes.

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:33.125
The arrow stays the same;
only the rulers we measure it with turn.

00:37:33.475 --> 00:37:39.725
Say the new x axis is turned by an angle
alpha, anticlockwise as seen from above.

00:37:40.075 --> 00:37:44.975
Its unit vector, x hat
prime, is cosine alpha, sine alpha.

00:37:45.325 --> 00:37:50.875
The new y axis, y hat prime, is
minus sine alpha, cosine alpha.

00:37:51.225 --> 00:37:55.275
The new components are
just dot products with the new arrows.

00:37:55.625 --> 00:37:59.650
So x prime is x cosine
alpha plus y sine alpha,

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:04.275
and y prime is minus x sine
alpha plus y cosine alpha.

00:38:04.625 --> 00:38:08.589
Let's try it on our east wind
of ten metres per second,

00:38:08.589 --> 00:38:11.400
with the axes turned by thirty degrees.

00:38:11.750 --> 00:38:17.200
The new x prime is ten times zero point
eight seven, about eight point seven.

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:23.950
And y prime is minus ten times
zero point five, which is minus five.

00:38:25.450 --> 00:38:29.900
And the length is still ten,
because the arrow itself didn't change.

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:36.050
We can write those two lines as a table
of numbers, called a rotation matrix.

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:38.875
Each entry is a direction cosine:

00:38:38.875 --> 00:38:43.225
the cosine of the angle between
an old axis and a new one.

00:38:43.575 --> 00:38:46.773
In the index notation
of the vectors movie,

00:38:46.773 --> 00:38:52.350
new component number j is the sum
over i of the old components times C i j.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:57.175
And here's a nice link: polar
components are just a rotation.

00:38:57.525 --> 00:39:01.095
At each place, r hat
and theta hat are the east and

00:39:01.095 --> 00:39:03.825
north arrows turned by the angle theta.

00:39:04.175 --> 00:39:06.206
So the wind's six outward and

00:39:06.206 --> 00:39:09.218
minus eight round were
just the east wind's

00:39:09.218 --> 00:39:12.650
components in axes
turned by fifty three degrees.

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:17.528
The only difference is that
in polar coordinates,

00:39:17.528 --> 00:39:20.700
the turning angle changes
from place to place.

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:26.000
Turning the axes is how forecasters
look at winds on a coast.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:30.982
What matters there is the part
of the wind that blows along the shore,

00:39:30.982 --> 00:39:35.150
and the part that blows across
it, onto the land or out to sea.

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:38.275
Here's the southwest coast of Florida.

00:39:38.625 --> 00:39:41.807
It runs from south-south-east
to north-north-west,

00:39:41.807 --> 00:39:45.625
at a bearing of about three
hundred and twenty nine degrees.

00:39:47.125 --> 00:39:51.384
Let's turn our axes so
that x prime runs along the coast,

00:39:51.384 --> 00:39:53.625
and y prime points out to sea.

00:39:53.975 --> 00:39:59.149
At eight in the evening, universal
time, about an hour after Ian came ashore,

00:39:59.149 --> 00:40:04.256
the model wind at Fort Myers Beach was
about thirty three metres per second,

00:40:04.256 --> 00:40:05.600
from the south-west.

00:40:07.100 --> 00:40:10.925
Along the shore, it's about
nine metres per second.

00:40:11.275 --> 00:40:16.475
Across the shore, it's about thirty two
metres per second, towards the land.

00:40:17.975 --> 00:40:21.800
Almost the whole wind was
pushing the sea onto the coast.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:26.100
That onshore push piles
water up against the coast.

00:40:26.450 --> 00:40:30.239
Mostly by that
push, and a little by the low pressure,

00:40:30.239 --> 00:40:32.975
the sea rose far above the normal tide.

00:40:33.325 --> 00:40:35.675
This is called a storm surge.

00:40:36.025 --> 00:40:37.419
At Fort Myers Beach,

00:40:37.419 --> 00:40:42.227
the official report found water ten
to fifteen feet above the ground:

00:40:42.227 --> 00:40:44.875
about three to four and a half metres.

00:40:45.225 --> 00:40:48.742
Now go about a hundred
and fifty kilometres north,

00:40:48.742 --> 00:40:52.400
to Tampa Bay, on the other
side of the storm's spin.

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:57.693
There, the coast runs almost due north,
and the wind was blowing out to sea,

00:40:57.693 --> 00:41:01.725
at about eleven and a half metres
per second across the shore.

00:41:03.225 --> 00:41:05.997
So the water was pushed
away from the coast,

00:41:05.997 --> 00:41:09.400
and the water level in the bay
fell instead of rising.

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:13.212
It's the same storm, on opposite
sides of the eye,

00:41:13.212 --> 00:41:16.050
and the across-shore part flips its sign.

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:19.525
Turned axes make that difference jump out.

00:41:19.875 --> 00:41:25.308
The wind along a coast matters too:
it can bring cold water up from below,

00:41:25.308 --> 00:41:28.025
which is a story for the Ekman movie.

00:41:28.825 --> 00:41:32.850
Our last change of coordinates
lets the origin move.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:36.587
Imagine you're on a train
gliding along a straight

00:41:36.587 --> 00:41:39.500
track at a steady twenty
metres per second.

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:43.800
You walk towards the front at one
and a half metres per second.

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:48.605
Someone standing beside the track
sees you moving at twenty plus one and

00:41:48.605 --> 00:41:51.575
a half: twenty one
and a half metres per second.

00:41:53.075 --> 00:41:55.100
So velocities add.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:41:59.800
The velocity seen from the ground
is the train's velocity,

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:04.000
capital U, plus the velocity
seen on the train, u prime.

00:42:04.350 --> 00:42:07.141
And positions shift as time goes on:

00:42:07.141 --> 00:42:10.707
x equals x prime
plus capital U times small t,

00:42:10.707 --> 00:42:13.575
while both clocks show the same time.

00:42:13.925 --> 00:42:16.713
Switching between two frames like this,

00:42:16.713 --> 00:42:19.858
moving at a steady
velocity without turning,

00:42:19.858 --> 00:42:24.075
is called a Galilean transformation,
after Galileo Galilei.

00:42:24.875 --> 00:42:27.025
Here's the beautiful part.

00:42:27.375 --> 00:42:29.875
What happens to accelerations?

00:42:30.225 --> 00:42:35.200
Let's take the rate of change
of u, which is capital U plus u prime.

00:42:35.550 --> 00:42:38.586
The rate of change of capital U is zero,

00:42:38.586 --> 00:42:41.850
because the train's
velocity never changes.

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:46.867
So the rate of change of u equals
the rate of change of u prime:

00:42:46.867 --> 00:42:50.075
the acceleration
is the same in both frames.

00:42:51.575 --> 00:42:55.463
Drop a ball on the smooth
train, and it falls straight down,

00:42:55.463 --> 00:42:59.545
speeding up by nine point eight
metres per second every second,

00:42:59.545 --> 00:43:02.850
exactly as it does
for the person beside the track.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:07.764
And since Newton's law says force
equals mass times acceleration,

00:43:07.764 --> 00:43:11.275
the same forces explain
the motion in both frames.

00:43:11.625 --> 00:43:15.788
For a fluid, the acceleration
of an air parcel is the local

00:43:15.788 --> 00:43:18.875
change of the wind
plus the advection term.

00:43:19.225 --> 00:43:24.050
Each piece can differ between the two
frames, but their sum is the same.

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:28.575
That's only true for a frame
moving steadily in a straight line.

00:43:28.925 --> 00:43:34.026
A frame that turns, like the spinning
Earth, adds new terms, the Coriolis and

00:43:34.026 --> 00:43:39.525
centrifugal effects, which we met in the
cross product movie on our spinning Earth.

00:43:40.325 --> 00:43:42.525
Now a real moving storm.

00:43:42.875 --> 00:43:45.771
Storm Ciarán crossed
the English Channel in the

00:43:45.771 --> 00:43:48.668
night from the first
to the second of November,

00:43:48.668 --> 00:43:49.900
twenty twenty-three.

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:53.195
Following its lowest
pressure hour by hour,

00:43:53.195 --> 00:43:56.278
the centre moved towards
the east-north-east,

00:43:56.278 --> 00:43:58.675
at about fifteen metres per second.

00:44:00.175 --> 00:44:04.907
Let's look at the winds on a ring two
hundred kilometres from the centre,

00:44:04.907 --> 00:44:07.500
at three in the morning, universal time.

00:44:07.850 --> 00:44:09.237
Seen from the ground,

00:44:09.237 --> 00:44:13.859
the winds on the south side averaged
about nineteen metres per second,

00:44:13.859 --> 00:44:16.500
but on the north side, only about eight.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.050
Why so lopsided?

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:25.694
On the south side, the storm's
anticlockwise spin blows towards the east,

00:44:25.694 --> 00:44:29.175
the same way the storm
moves, so the two add up.

00:44:29.525 --> 00:44:33.117
On the north side, the spin
blows towards the west,

00:44:33.117 --> 00:44:36.075
against the motion, so they partly cancel.

00:44:36.425 --> 00:44:40.750
Now let's subtract the storm's
own velocity from every wind.

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:46.225
These are the storm-relative winds: what
you'd feel riding along with the storm.

00:44:47.025 --> 00:44:49.750
So what changes, and what doesn't?

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:54.600
The wind at each point changes
by fifteen metres per second.

00:44:54.950 --> 00:44:57.956
But the average spin
round the ring is about

00:44:57.956 --> 00:45:00.825
fourteen metres per second in both frames.

00:45:02.325 --> 00:45:07.425
And the average inflow is about six
metres per second in both frames.

00:45:08.925 --> 00:45:10.925
That's no accident.

00:45:11.275 --> 00:45:15.018
A steady, uniform wind blows
as much round one side of

00:45:15.018 --> 00:45:17.984
a circle as it blows back round the other,

00:45:17.984 --> 00:45:22.575
so it adds nothing to the average
spin, or to the average inflow.

00:45:22.925 --> 00:45:28.100
The spin belongs to the storm, and the
motion belongs to the whole pattern.

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:33.069
For a house on the coast, though, the
ground-relative wind is what counts,

00:45:33.069 --> 00:45:37.375
and that's why the strongest winds
came on the storm's southern side.

00:45:37.725 --> 00:45:42.798
That night, the weather station at Brest
measured a gust of eighty four knots,

00:45:42.798 --> 00:45:46.050
about a hundred and fifty
six kilometres per hour.

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:51.350
The moving frame also explains
what a fixed barometer sees.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:54.777
Picture the pressure
pattern as a frozen map,

00:45:54.777 --> 00:45:58.400
sliding east-north-east
at fifteen metres per second.

00:45:58.750 --> 00:46:03.618
A barometer that never moves sees
the pressure fall as the low approaches,

00:46:03.618 --> 00:46:06.775
even if the storm itself
isn't deepening at all.

00:46:07.125 --> 00:46:09.936
Changing to the moving
frame splits the rate

00:46:09.936 --> 00:46:12.619
of change at a fixed place into two parts:

00:46:12.619 --> 00:46:15.878
the change seen by someone
riding with the pattern,

00:46:15.878 --> 00:46:19.775
minus the pattern's velocity
times the slope of the pressure.

00:46:20.125 --> 00:46:23.471
At Plymouth, on England's
south coast, at midnight,

00:46:23.471 --> 00:46:28.325
the pressure was falling by about
three point eight hectopascals per hour.

00:46:29.825 --> 00:46:34.425
The sliding pattern alone accounts
for about three point two of them.

00:46:35.925 --> 00:46:40.975
The remaining zero point six
is the storm itself changing as it moved.

00:46:41.325 --> 00:46:45.725
So most of what the barometer
saw was the storm just arriving.

00:46:46.075 --> 00:46:50.028
This is the same idea as advection
in the dot product movie,

00:46:50.028 --> 00:46:53.850
and we'll build it fully
in the material derivative movie.

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:58.572
To sum up, coordinates are
numbers that pin down a place,

00:46:58.572 --> 00:47:01.875
measured from an origin
along chosen directions.

00:47:02.225 --> 00:47:05.500
Cartesian unit vectors
are the same everywhere,

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:09.750
while polar, cylindrical
and spherical ones turn as you move.

00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:14.400
The arc length is the radius
times the angle, in radians.

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:16.574
Round Hurricane Ian's eye,

00:47:16.574 --> 00:47:19.590
polar coordinates split
the wind into about

00:47:19.590 --> 00:47:22.536
thirty seven metres per second of spin and

00:47:22.536 --> 00:47:24.500
twelve and a half of inflow.

00:47:24.850 --> 00:47:30.175
And because theta hat turns, a steady
spin still accelerates towards the eye.

00:47:30.525 --> 00:47:34.763
On the Earth, a degree of latitude
is always about a hundred and

00:47:34.763 --> 00:47:35.956
eleven kilometres,

00:47:35.956 --> 00:47:40.790
but a degree of longitude is the radius
times cosine phi times the angle:

00:47:40.790 --> 00:47:44.962
seventy four kilometres
at Brest, and twenty three at Svalbard,

00:47:44.962 --> 00:47:48.075
because the parallels
shrink towards the poles.

00:47:48.425 --> 00:47:50.483
Areas go with cosine phi too,

00:47:50.483 --> 00:47:55.025
so half of the Earth lies between
thirty south and thirty north.

00:47:55.375 --> 00:47:58.250
The shortest route is a great circle.

00:47:58.600 --> 00:48:03.950
The Mercator map keeps shapes and
bearings, and equal-area maps keep sizes.

00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:08.223
Turning the axes gives new
components of the same arrow,

00:48:08.223 --> 00:48:10.325
like along and across a coast.

00:48:10.675 --> 00:48:15.816
And a Galilean change to a steadily
moving frame changes velocities,

00:48:15.816 --> 00:48:20.050
but leaves accelerations,
and a storm's spin, unchanged.

00:48:20.850 --> 00:48:25.008
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

00:48:25.008 --> 00:48:27.050
so watch out for the others.

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:31.797
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

00:48:31.797 --> 00:48:35.450
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:40.628
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

00:48:40.628 --> 00:48:43.450
are on the website that
goes with this video.
